window.PROBLEMS = (window.PROBLEMS || []).concat([
  { id:"LT001", domain:"literature", title:"Hamlet Soliloquy Purpose", difficulty:"easy", tags:["drama"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"From William Shakespeare's Hamlet:\n\nTo be, or not to be, that is the question:\nWhether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer\nThe slings and arrows of outrageous fortune,\nOr to take arms against a sea of troubles\nAnd by opposing end them. To die—to sleep,\nNo more; and by a sleep to say we end\nThe heart-ache and the thousand natural shocks\nThat flesh is heir to: 'tis a consummation\nDevoutly to be wish'd. To die, to sleep;\nTo sleep, perchance to dream—ay, there's the rub:\nFor in that sleep of death what dreams may come\nWhen we have shuffled off this mortal coil,\nMust give us pause.",
    stem:"In this soliloquy, Hamlet is primarily—",
    choices:["giving battle orders to Fortinbras","weighing whether enduring life is better than risking death","consoling himself that death is only a dreamless sleep, with no remaining doubt","mapping Denmark's political geography as the real question"], answer:1,
    explanation:"The opening antithesis (to be, or not to be) frames a choice between suffering life and ending it, then hesitates over dreams after death. He is not commanding an army. Dreamless sleep is what he hopes, then doubts ('there's the rub'). Geography is not the question." },

  { id:"LT002", domain:"literature", title:"Shakespearean Sonnet Form", difficulty:"easy", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    passage:"William Shakespeare, Sonnet 18:\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.",
    stem:"This poem is best identified as a Shakespearean sonnet because it—",
    choices:["has fourteen lines in iambic pentameter with a closing couplet","is a Petrarchan sonnet built as octave plus sestet with no couplet","is blank verse: unrhymed iambic pentameter throughout","is a fourteen-line poem in free verse with no regular meter or rhyme"], answer:0,
    explanation:"A Shakespearean (English) sonnet has 14 lines, typically iambic pentameter, rhyme scheme abab cdcd efef gg, and a final couplet. Petrarchan form is 8+6. Blank verse is unrhymed pentameter (often in plays). Free verse lacks this regular rhyme and meter." },

  { id:"LT003", domain:"literature", title:"Alliteration in Blake", difficulty:"easy", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    passage:"From William Blake, 'The Tyger' (Songs of Experience). The speaker questions what maker could shape a creature of such symmetry and terror:\n\nTyger Tyger, burning bright,\nIn the forests of the night;\nWhat immortal hand or eye,\nCould frame thy fearful symmetry?\n\nIn what distant deeps or skies\nBurnt the fire of thine eyes?\nOn what wings dare he aspire?\nWhat the hand, dare seize the fire?\n\nAnd what shoulder, and what art,\nCould twist the sinews of thy heart?\nAnd when thy heart began to beat,\nWhat dread hand? and what dread feet?\n\nWhen the stars threw down their spears\nAnd water'd heaven with their tears:\nDid he smile his work to see?\nDid he who made the Lamb make thee?",
    stem:"Tyger Tyger, burning bright is the clearest example of—",
    choices:["alliteration","end rhyme only, with no repeated consonant sounds","assonance on the long i of Tyger and bright","a refrain that returns at the close of every stanza"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Alliteration repeats initial consonant sounds (burning / bright). The stanza also rhymes, but the quoted phrase's standout device is the b-repeat. Assonance would be a vowel echo; a refrain is a returning line, not this opening pair." },

  { id:"LT004", domain:"literature", title:"Metaphor in Sonnet 18", difficulty:"easy", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    passage:"William Shakespeare, Sonnet 18:\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.",
    stem:"The eye of heaven is best read as a metaphor for—",
    choices:["the moon, whose gold complexion is dimmed","the sun","a human spy watching the beloved","death, who later brags in the shade"], answer:1,
    explanation:"Too hot and gold complexion point to the sun, not the cooler moon. The figure is not a person watching; death appears later as a separate personification, not as the eye of heaven." },

  { id:"LT005", domain:"literature", title:"Theme versus Plot", difficulty:"easy", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    stem:"In a fable, a fox cannot reach grapes and then calls them sour. Which statement is a theme rather than a plot event?",
    choices:["The fox jumped at the grapes and missed.","The fox walked away still hungry.","People sometimes scorn what they cannot have.","The grapes hung high on a vine."], answer:2,
    explanation:"Theme is an insight about life. The other choices retell events (plot). Tests often offer plot summaries disguised as 'the meaning.'" },

  { id:"LT006", domain:"literature", title:"Apostrophe in Dickinson", difficulty:"medium", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    passage:"Emily Dickinson's lyric presents Death as a caller who takes the speaker on a slow carriage ride past ordinary scenes of a life:\n\nBecause I could not stop for Death –\nHe kindly stopped for me –\nThe Carriage held but just Ourselves –\nAnd Immortality.\n\nWe slowly drove – He knew no haste\nAnd I had put away\nMy labor and my leisure too,\nFor His Civility –\n\nWe passed the School, where Children strove\nAt Recess – in the Ring –\nWe passed the Fields of Gazing Grain –\nWe passed the Setting Sun –\n\nOr rather – He passed Us –\nThe Dews drew quivering and Chill –\nFor only Gossamer, my Gown –\nMy Tippet – only Tulle –",
    stem:"Treating Death as a courteous carriage driver is an example of—",
    choices:["apostrophe and personification","historical documentation of a real carriage ride","a metaphor that never gives Death human manners","allegory in which Immortality is only a place name"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Personification gives Death human manners; presenting Death as a companion is closely related to apostrophe (addressing an abstract or absent figure). The lyric is not a historical record. Kindly and Civility are human manners. Immortality rides as a figure, not a mapped town." },

  { id:"LT007", domain:"literature", title:"Aside versus Soliloquy", difficulty:"medium", tags:["drama"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    stem:"In a play, a character speaks at length while alone on stage, revealing private thoughts the other characters cannot hear. This convention is called a—",
    choices:["an aside, because another character is still on stage","soliloquy","dramatic irony, because the audience already knows the secret","a chorus speech summarizing offstage action"], answer:1,
    explanation:"A soliloquy is extended speech to oneself (or the audience) when others are absent or ignored. An aside is usually a brief remark with others present. Dramatic irony is a knowledge gap, not a speech type. A chorus comments as a group, not as this lone speaker." },

  { id:"LT008", domain:"literature", title:"Dramatic Irony in Romeo and Juliet", difficulty:"medium", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From the Prologue to William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet:\n\nTwo households, both alike in dignity,\nIn fair Verona, where we lay our scene,\nFrom ancient grudge break to new mutiny,\nWhere civil blood makes civil hands unclean.\nFrom forth the fatal loins of these two foes\nA pair of star-cross'd lovers take their life;\nWhose misadventured piteous overthrows\nDo with their death bury their parents' strife.\nThe fearful passage of their death-mark'd love,\nAnd the continuance of their parents' rage,\nWhich, but their children's end, nought could remove,\nIs now the two hours' traffic of our stage.",
    stem:"The prologue creates dramatic irony mainly because—",
    choices:["the audience is told the lovers will die before the action unfolds","the families, not the lovers, are the only characters who will speak","the play has no conflict once the grudge is named","alike in dignity means the families share every belief"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Dramatic irony exists when the audience knows more than the characters. Star-cross'd lovers take their life spoils the ending. Characters do speak; conflict is central; alike in dignity is social rank, not identical beliefs." },

  { id:"LT009", domain:"literature", title:"Couplet Function", difficulty:"easy", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    passage:"William Shakespeare, Sonnet 18. The speaker first tests a comparison to summer, then claims that verse will keep the beloved's 'eternal summer':\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.",
    stem:"The final two lines are best described as a couplet that—",
    choices:["states the poem's resolving claim: verse outlasts death","introduces a new speaker who rejects the comparison to summer","changes the rhyme scheme into ballad quatrains (abcb)","cancels the earlier praise by admitting summer's lease is short"], answer:0,
    explanation:"The gg couplet in a Shakespearean sonnet often turns or clinches the argument. Here this (the poem) gives life. No new speaker appears. Ballad stanza is a different form. Summer's short lease is earlier; the couplet does not retract the praise." },

  { id:"LT010", domain:"literature", title:"Assonance in Poe", difficulty:"medium", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven':\n\nOnce upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,\nOver many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore—\nWhile I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,\nAs of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.\n''Tis some visitor,' I muttered, 'tapping at my chamber door—\nOnly this and nothing more.'\n\nAh, distinctly I remember it was in the bleak December,\nAnd each separate dying ember wrought its ghost upon the floor.",
    stem:"Weak and weary most clearly illustrates—",
    choices:["assonance (repeated vowel sound)","alliteration on w, with no shared vowel","a volta turning from octave to sestet","consonance only, because weary ends on a different vowel"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Assonance repeats vowel sounds (ea in weak/weary). Initial w is alliteration as well, but the item asks what the paired vowels most clearly illustrate. A volta is a sonnet turn; this is not a sonnet octave. The shared vowel is the point, not a claim that consonants are absent." },

  { id:"LT011", domain:"literature", title:"Fable Moral", difficulty:"easy", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    passage:"In the Aesop tradition, a hungry fox saw ripe grapes hanging from a high trellis beside a dusty road. He leaped once, twice, and a third time, but the cluster stayed out of reach. Dust stuck to his paws; a pair of sparrows chattered as if laughing. At last he turned away and said, as if to comfort himself, 'They are sour, and not worth the taking.' A watching crow remarked that it is easier to scorn a prize than to admit defeat. The fox flicked his tail and trotted into the hedge, still hungry, repeating the word sour as though saying it could make the grapes change. Travelers who heard the tale were meant to notice the excuse, not the grapes.",
    stem:"This narrative is best classified as a fable because it—",
    choices:["uses animals and ends with a moral lesson","is a lyric that argues by metaphor rather than plot","is a factual report of a single fox's behavior","has no implied lesson, only a description of hunger"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Fables (as in the Aesop tradition) typically feature animals and teach a moral. This is plotted narrative, not a lyric or a naturalist's report, and the crow's remark plus the fox's excuse supply the lesson." },

  { id:"LT012", domain:"literature", title:"Romantic Emphasis", difficulty:"easy", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    passage:"From Walt Whitman, 'Song of Myself':\n\nI celebrate myself, and sing myself,\nAnd what I assume you shall assume,\nFor every atom belonging to me as good belongs to you.\nI loafe and invite my soul,\nI lean and loafe at my ease observing a spear of summer grass.\nMy tongue, every atom of my blood, form'd from this soil, this air,\nBorn here of parents born here from parents the same, and their parents the same,\nI, now thirty-seven years old in perfect health begin,\nHoping to cease not till death.",
    stem:"The passage most clearly reflects a Romantic-era emphasis on—",
    choices:["the individual self, the body, and nature","courtly satire of marriage and inherited fortune","naturalist attention to heredity as a trap, without celebration","Realist inventory of ordinary social manners"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Whitman celebrates self, soul, grass, soil, and air—Romantic/Transcendental valuing of individual and nature. Marriage-market satire, grim heredity, and manners-inventory are other period modes." },

  { id:"LT013", domain:"literature", title:"Austen Irony of Narration", difficulty:"medium", tags:["prose"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"From Jane Austen, Pride and Prejudice:\n\nIt is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.\n\nHowever little known the feelings or views of such a man may be on his first entering a neighbourhood, this truth is so well fixed in the minds of the surrounding families, that he is considered as the rightful property of some one or other of their daughters.\n\n'My dear Mr. Bennet,' said his lady to him one day, 'have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?'",
    stem:"The opening sentence is best described as—",
    choices:["verbal irony that mocks a social assumption","a sincere thesis the narrator will prove with data","an epic invocation asking a muse to justify marriage","free indirect style that is already inside Mr. Bennet's mind"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Universally acknowledged overstates a neighborhood prejudice; Austen invites readers to doubt the 'truth.' It is not a scientific proof, an epic prayer, or yet Mr. Bennet's interior—the next paragraphs show the families' minds, not his." },

  { id:"LT014", domain:"literature", title:"Internal Rhyme in The Raven", difficulty:"medium", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven.' A sleepless student sits among old books when a sound at the door breaks the silence:\n\nOnce upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,\nOver many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore—\nWhile I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,\nAs of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.\n''Tis some visitor,' I muttered, 'tapping at my chamber door—\nOnly this and nothing more.'\n\nAh, distinctly I remember it was in the bleak December,\nAnd each separate dying ember wrought its ghost upon the floor.",
    stem:"Dreary / weary and napping / tapping are examples of—",
    choices:["internal rhyme (and end rhyme) used for incantatory sound","assonance only, with no matching end sounds","prose paragraphing that drops line breaks","a refrain, because nothing more returns as a closing formula"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Poe rhymes within and at the ends of lines to create a chant-like effect. Assonance is vowel echo; these pairs also rhyme. The lines are verse, not prose. Nothing more is refrain-like, but dreary/weary and napping/tapping are the rhyme pattern asked." },

  { id:"LT015", domain:"literature", title:"Iambic Pentameter", difficulty:"medium", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    passage:"William Shakespeare, Sonnet 18. Read the opening line in the context of the whole English sonnet:\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.",
    stem:"Scanned as a typical Shakespearean line, the meter is—",
    choices:["iambic pentameter (five iambs)","trochaic tetrameter, as in much of 'The Tyger'","anapestic trimeter typical of a light comic stanza","free verse with no recurring beat"], answer:0,
    explanation:"An iamb is unstressed-stressed; pentameter has five feet: Shall I | com-PARE | thee TO | a SUM | mer's DAY. Trochees reverse the iamb; anapests are two unstressed plus stressed. The sonnet is metrical, not free verse." },

  { id:"LT016", domain:"literature", title:"Simile versus Metaphor", difficulty:"easy", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    stem:"Which line contains a simile?",
    choices:["Hope is the thing with feathers.","My love is like a red, red rose.","The curtain of night fell.","Death stopped his carriage."], answer:1,
    explanation:"A simile uses like or as. A is a metaphor (Dickinson-style), C is a metaphor, D is personification. (The rose line is the traditional Burns example; the skill is the like marker.)" },

  { id:"LT017", domain:"literature", title:"Tone in Douglass", difficulty:"medium", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From Frederick Douglass, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass:\n\nThe more I read, the more I was led to abhor and detest my enslavers. I could regard them in no other light than a band of successful robbers, who had left their homes, and gone to Africa, and stolen us from our homes, and in a strange land reduced us to slavery. I loathed them as being the meanest as well as the most wicked of men. As I read and contemplated the subject, behold! that very discontentment which Master Hugh had predicted would follow my learning to read had already come, to torment and sting my soul to unutterable anguish. As I writhed under it, I would at times feel that learning to read had been a curse rather than a blessing.",
    stem:"The tone of this passage is best described as—",
    choices:["detached amusement at Master Hugh's prediction","moral outrage mixed with painful irony","neutral inventory of what the speaker read","nostalgic fondness for the enslavers he names"], answer:1,
    explanation:"Abhor, detest, robbers, loathed, anguish, and the curse of literacy create outrage and bitter irony (learning brings pain). It is not amusement, a book list, or fondness." },

  { id:"LT018", domain:"literature", title:"Romanticism versus Realism", difficulty:"hard", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    stem:"Which pairing best contrasts Romanticism with literary Realism?",
    choices:["Romanticism: intense feeling, nature, and imagination; Realism: ordinary social life and plausible motive","Romanticism: only laboratory reports; Realism: only sonnets","Romanticism: medieval religious allegory; Realism: oral trickster tales","They are identical movements distinguished only by century labels"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Romantic writers (Blake, Whitman) elevate emotion, nature, and the visionary self. Realist fiction stresses plausible social worlds. The other pairings scramble genres and eras." },

  { id:"LT019", domain:"literature", title:"Duncan's Pleasant Seat", difficulty:"medium", tags:["drama"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"In Shakespeare's Macbeth, King Duncan arrives at Macbeth's castle, unaware that Macbeth and Lady Macbeth intend to murder him.\n\nDUNCAN: This castle hath a pleasant seat; the air\nNimbly and sweetly recommends itself\nUnto our gentle senses.\nBANQUO: This guest of summer,\nThe temple-haunting martlet, does approve,\nBy his loved mansionry, that the heaven's breath\nSmells wooingly here: no jutty, frieze,\nButtress, nor coign of vantage, but this bird\nHath made his pendent bed and procreant cradle:\nWhere they most breed and haunt, I have observed,\nThe air is delicate.",
    stem:"Duncan's praise of the castle air is an example of dramatic irony because—",
    choices:["the audience already knows the hosts plan his death","Duncan is alone in a soliloquy with no listeners","the martlet's nesting proves the castle is actually safe","Banquo's agreement means there is no gap between appearance and threat"], answer:0,
    explanation:"We know what Duncan does not: the 'pleasant' castle is a trap. He is speaking with Banquo, not soliloquizing. The martlet is a swallow-like bird whose 'approval' deepens the irony; agreement does not cancel the audience's knowledge." },

  { id:"LT020", domain:"literature", title:"Aesop and Oral Tradition", difficulty:"easy", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    passage:"In the Aesop tradition, a hungry fox saw ripe grapes hanging from a high trellis beside a dusty road. He leaped once, twice, and a third time, but the cluster stayed out of reach. Dust stuck to his paws; a pair of sparrows chattered as if laughing. At last he turned away and said, as if to comfort himself, 'They are sour, and not worth the taking.' A watching crow remarked that it is easier to scorn a prize than to admit defeat. The fox flicked his tail and trotted into the hedge, still hungry, repeating the word sour as though saying it could make the grapes change. Travelers who heard the tale were meant to notice the excuse, not the grapes.",
    stem:"Fables of this kind are associated with the Aesop tradition mainly because they—",
    choices:["travel as brief moral tales, often with animal characters, across cultures","must use Shakespearean sonnet form to count as literature","survive only as modern copyrighted novels that cannot be retold","cannot be retold without destroying the moral"], answer:0,
    explanation:"The Aesopic tradition is oral-to-written world literature: short, portable morals. Form is not a sonnet; retelling is how they survive, not a copyright barrier." },

  { id:"LT021", domain:"literature", title:"Petrarchan versus Shakespearean", difficulty:"medium", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    passage:"Elizabeth Barrett Browning, Sonnet 43, from Sonnets from the Portuguese:\n\nHow do I love thee? Let me count the ways.\nI love thee to the depth and breadth and height\nMy soul can reach, when feeling out of sight\nFor the ends of being and ideal grace.\nI love thee to the level of every day's\nMost quiet need, by sun and candle-light.\nI love thee freely, as men strive for right;\nI love thee purely, as they turn from praise.\nI love thee with the passion put to use\nIn my old griefs, and with my childhood's faith.\nI love thee with a love I seemed to lose\nWith my lost saints. I love thee with the breath,\nSmiles, tears, of all my life; and, if God choose,\nI shall but love thee better after death.",
    stem:"Compared with a Shakespearean sonnet, this poem is closer to the Petrarchan (Italian) pattern because it—",
    choices:["organizes as an octave plus sestet rather than three quatrains and a couplet","follows Shakespearean abab cdcd efef gg with a closing couplet","is unrhymed dramatic dialogue in blank verse","is a prose fable whose moral is counted in 'ways'"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Barrett Browning's sequence uses Petrarchan structure (abba abba then a sestet). Shakespearean form is 4-4-4-2 with a couplet. It is still a 14-line lyric, not a play or a fable." },

  { id:"LT022", domain:"literature", title:"Onomatopoeia and Repetition", difficulty:"medium", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven.' The speaker, half-asleep over forgotten books, tries to explain a sound at the door:\n\nOnce upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,\nOver many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore—\nWhile I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,\nAs of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.\n''Tis some visitor,' I muttered, 'tapping at my chamber door—\nOnly this and nothing more.'\n\nAh, distinctly I remember it was in the bleak December,",
    stem:"Tapping and rapping function as—",
    choices:["onomatopoeia and repetition that imitate the sound of knocking","alliteration only, because the words share no sound with a knock","an aside whispered to an offstage visitor","an epic simile comparing the sound to forgotten lore"], answer:0,
    explanation:"The words mimic knock-sounds (onomatopoeia) and repeat for emphasis. Alliteration would be initial consonants; these words imitate the noise. There is no aside to a visitor he has not seen. Forgotten lore is the books, not an extended like/as comparison of the tapping." },

  { id:"LT023", domain:"literature", title:"Personification of Death", difficulty:"medium", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    passage:"Emily Dickinson's lyric presents Death as a caller who takes the speaker on a slow carriage ride:\n\nBecause I could not stop for Death –\nHe kindly stopped for me –\nThe Carriage held but just Ourselves –\nAnd Immortality.\n\nWe slowly drove – He knew no haste\nAnd I had put away\nMy labor and my leisure too,\nFor His Civility –\n\nWe passed the School, where Children strove\nAt Recess – in the Ring –\nWe passed the Fields of Gazing Grain –\nWe passed the Setting Sun –",
    stem:"Kindly and Civility applied to Death mainly create—",
    choices:["personification that makes Death a polite suitor or driver","a legal contract in which Immortality is the witness","blank verse drama spoken by two living characters","a weather report of the setting sun and chill dew"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Human courtesy attributed to Death is personification (and a chilling irony). The poem is lyric, not a deed or a play. Sun and dew are setting details, not the work of kindly and Civility." },

  { id:"LT024", domain:"literature", title:"Setting in The Raven", difficulty:"medium", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven':\n\nOnce upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,\nOver many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore—\nWhile I nodded, nearly napping, suddenly there came a tapping,\nAs of some one gently rapping, rapping at my chamber door.\n''Tis some visitor,' I muttered, 'tapping at my chamber door—\nOnly this and nothing more.'\n\nAh, distinctly I remember it was in the bleak December,\nAnd each separate dying ember wrought its ghost upon the floor.",
    stem:"The setting that most shapes the mood is—",
    choices:["a bright marketplace at noon among the forgotten lore","a lonely chamber at midnight in December","a comic farmyard at dawn when the tapping begins","a crowded election rally in bleak December"], answer:1,
    explanation:"Midnight, dreary, bleak December, and a dying ember build Gothic isolation. Marketplace, farmyard, and rally contradict the chamber-and-ember details a skimmer might still lift (lore, tapping, December)." },

  { id:"LT025", domain:"literature", title:"Oral Tale Features", difficulty:"easy", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    stem:"Which feature is most typical of oral-tradition tales (including many fables and folktales)?",
    choices:["stock characters, repetition, and a portable moral or lesson","a unique psychological portrait of each animal, with no repeated patterns","a requirement that every retelling keep identical wording","a requirement that animal characters never appear"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Oral tales favor memorable patterns and lessons for retelling. Unique psychology and frozen wording are print-culture or copyright habits, not oral tradition. Animals are common, not forbidden." },

  { id:"LT026", domain:"literature", title:"Cry of the Children", difficulty:"hard", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From Elizabeth Barrett Browning, 'The Cry of the Children':\n\nDo ye hear the children weeping, O my brothers,\nEre the sorrow comes with years?\nThey are leaning their young heads against their mothers,\nAnd that cannot stop their tears.\nThe young lambs are bleating in the meadows,\nThe young birds are chirping in the nest,\nThe young fawns are playing with the shadows,\nThe young flowers are blowing toward the west—\nBut the young, young children, O my brothers,\nThey are weeping bitterly!\nThey are weeping in the playtime of the others,\nIn the country of the free.",
    stem:"The poem's social critique is best described as—",
    choices:["contrasting free animal young with laboring children to protest child labor","celebrating factory owners as shepherds of the young","arguing that children should never play, even in meadows","describing a royal wedding in the country of the free"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Lambs, birds, and fawns enjoy youth while children weep 'in the playtime of the others'—a Victorian protest against industrial child labor. It does not praise owners, ban play, or narrate a wedding. O my brothers is apostrophe to the public." },

  { id:"LT027", domain:"literature", title:"Equiano as Witness", difficulty:"medium", tags:["prose"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"From Olaudah Equiano, The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano:\n\nThe closeness of the place, and the heat of the climate, added to the number in the ship, which was so crowded that each had scarcely room to turn himself, almost suffocated us. This produced copious perspirations, so that the air soon became unfit for respiration, from a variety of loathsome smells, and brought on a sickness among the slaves, of which many died, thus falling victims to the improvident avarice, as I may call it, of their purchasers. This wretched situation was again aggravated by the galling of the chains, now become insupportable; and the filth of the necessary tubs, into which the children often fell, and were almost suffocated. The shrieks of the women, and the groans of the dying, rendered the whole a scene of horror almost inconceivable.",
    stem:"Equiano's first-person account functions primarily as—",
    choices:["eyewitness testimony meant to make the Middle Passage morally undeniable","a comic sea narrative that treats crowding as inconvenience","an instruction manual for improving ship ventilation","a lyric of private grief with no public argument"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Sensory detail and the charge of avarice serve abolitionist witness. The passage is horror, not comedy, a how-to, or lyric form." },

  { id:"LT028", domain:"literature", title:"Volta in Sonnet 18", difficulty:"medium", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    passage:"William Shakespeare, Sonnet 18:\n\nShall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.",
    stem:"The turn (volta) toward the beloved's lasting summer begins most clearly at—",
    choices:["Shall I compare thee","Rough winds do shake","But thy eternal summer shall not fade","So long as men can breathe, which only restates summer's short lease"], answer:2,
    explanation:"But signals the shift from summer's flaws to the claim that poetry preserves the beloved. Line 1 is the question; rough winds continue the summer catalog. The couplet clinches; it does not return to summer's lease." },

  { id:"LT029", domain:"literature", title:"Refrain Function", difficulty:"medium", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven.' After the bird has entered and spoken its one word, the speaker imagines the shadow as a permanent stain on the soul:\n\nAnd the Raven, never flitting, still is sitting, still is sitting\nOn the pallid bust of Pallas just above my chamber door;\nAnd his eyes have all the seeming of a demon's that is dreaming,\nAnd the lamp-light o'er him streaming throws his shadow on the floor;\nAnd my soul from out that shadow that lies floating on the floor\nShall be lifted—nevermore!\n\nThe repeated last word has already answered every plea for hope earlier in the poem; here it closes the scene on the bust of Pallas, goddess of wisdom, now a perch for despair.",
    stem:"Nevermore, returning through the poem, is best classified as a—",
    choices:["refrain that hammers the theme of unending loss","internal rhyme like dreary/weary, not a returning line","a stage direction telling the actor to exit","an apostrophe addressing Pallas rather than repeating a word"], answer:0,
    explanation:"A refrain is a repeated line or phrase. Here it seals despair. Internal rhyme is a different sound device in the poem. It is not blocking. Pallas is named; nevermore is the repeated closer, not an address to the bust." },

  { id:"LT030", domain:"literature", title:"Apostrophe in Barrett Browning", difficulty:"medium", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    passage:"From Elizabeth Barrett Browning, 'The Cry of the Children,' a Victorian protest against industrial child labor. The speaker calls a national audience to hear what playtime should have been:\n\nDo ye hear the children weeping, O my brothers,\nEre the sorrow comes with years?\nThey are leaning their young heads against their mothers,\nAnd that cannot stop their tears.\nThe young lambs are bleating in the meadows,\nThe young birds are chirping in the nest,\nThe young fawns are playing with the shadows,\nThe young flowers are blowing toward the west—\nBut the young, young children, O my brothers,\nThey are weeping bitterly!",
    stem:"O my brothers is an example of apostrophe because the speaker—",
    choices:["directly addresses an audience (or nation) as if present","lists factory machines in order of danger","quotes a timetable of the children's workday hours","describes iambic pentameter rather than appealing to hearers"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Apostrophe addresses absent, abstract, or collective hearers. O plus brothers calls the public to witness. Machines, hours, and meter may be in the poem's world, but this phrase is direct address." },

  { id:"LT031", domain:"literature", title:"Trickster Pattern", difficulty:"easy", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    stem:"In many world tales, a smaller or weaker character outwits a stronger one. This recurring role is often called a—",
    choices:["trickster","chorus","volta","soliloquy"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Tricksters (Anansi, Coyote, some Aesopic foxes) use cunning. Chorus, volta, and soliloquy are other literary terms a test-taker may swap with character type." },

  { id:"LT032", domain:"literature", title:"Unreliable-Adjacent Narrator", difficulty:"medium", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Tell-Tale Heart':\n\nTRUE!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.\n\nIt is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!",
    stem:"Readers should treat this first-person voice as—",
    choices:["an omniscient, unbiased chronicle of the old man's thoughts","a self-justifying narrator whose sanity claims clash with obsessive detail","the police report filed before the crime, in official third person","Austen's ironic narrator, who mocks neighborhood marriage plots"], answer:1,
    explanation:"He insists he is not mad while describing haunting, hell-hearing, and fixation on an eye—classic unreliable or severely limited POV. It is not omniscient, a pre-crime report, or Austen's social irony." },

  { id:"LT033", domain:"literature", title:"Gothic Features", difficulty:"medium", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Raven.' Grief for a lost woman named Lenore fills a winter room:\n\nOnce upon a midnight dreary, while I pondered, weak and weary,\nOver many a quaint and curious volume of forgotten lore—\nAh, distinctly I remember it was in the bleak December,\nAnd each separate dying ember wrought its ghost upon the floor.\nEagerly I wished the morrow;—vainly I had sought to borrow\nFrom my books surcease of sorrow—sorrow for the lost Lenore—\nFor the rare and radiant maiden whom the angels name Lenore—\nNameless here for evermore.",
    stem:"These lines most clearly show Gothic (Dark Romantic) features through—",
    choices:["gloom, a haunting memory, and supernatural coloring of ordinary objects","a sunny picnic inventory of forgotten lore","a factory-reform petition on behalf of Lenore","comic epistolary wit about a lost engagement"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Bleak December, ghost upon the floor, and grief for Lenore are Gothic mood and obsession. Picnic, petition, and comedy contradict the diction even when they borrow a noun from the lines." },

  { id:"LT034", domain:"literature", title:"Douglass and Literacy", difficulty:"medium", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From Frederick Douglass, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass:\n\nThe more I read, the more I was led to abhor and detest my enslavers. I could regard them in no other light than a band of successful robbers, who had left their homes, and gone to Africa, and stolen us from our homes, and in a strange land reduced us to slavery. I loathed them as being the meanest as well as the most wicked of men. As I read and contemplated the subject, behold! that very discontentment which Master Hugh had predicted would follow my learning to read had already come, to torment and sting my soul to unutterable anguish.",
    stem:"In historical context, this passage argues that literacy was dangerous to slavery because it—",
    choices:["helped the enslaved person recognize injustice and desire freedom","taught only polite accomplishments useful to enslavers","was illegal for enslavers to practice, not for the enslaved","had no political meaning once Master Hugh predicted discontent"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Master Hugh predicted discontent from reading; Douglass confirms that knowledge exposes robbery and wickedness. Slave narratives treat literacy as a path to political consciousness, not etiquette or neutrality." },

  { id:"LT035", domain:"literature", title:"Hamlet's Rub", difficulty:"hard", tags:["drama"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"From William Shakespeare's Hamlet. Alone, the prince weighs endurance against death, then finds a new obstacle:\n\nTo be, or not to be, that is the question:\nWhether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer\nThe slings and arrows of outrageous fortune,\nOr to take arms against a sea of troubles\nAnd by opposing end them. To die—to sleep,\nNo more; and by a sleep to say we end\nThe heart-ache and the thousand natural shocks\nThat flesh is heir to: 'tis a consummation\nDevoutly to be wish'd. To die, to sleep;\nTo sleep, perchance to dream—ay, there's the rub:\nFor in that sleep of death what dreams may come\nWhen we have shuffled off this mortal coil,\nMust give us pause.",
    stem:"There's the rub indicates that Hamlet hesitates because—",
    choices:["the afterlife (dreams after death) is unknown and possibly worse than life","he has already decided to take arms and needs only a ship","Ophelia has finished the speech, so the question is closed","the line is a stage direction to pause the action for a curtain"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Rub means obstacle. The unknown dreams of death create pause. He is still reasoning, not traveling, yielding the speech, or calling a cue." },

  { id:"LT036", domain:"literature", title:"Ballad versus Sonnet", difficulty:"hard", tags:["poetry"], skillIds:["poetry-forms"],
    stem:"A narrative poem in quatrains with a strong beat and a refrain, often sung, is best classified as a—",
    choices:["ballad","Shakespearean sonnet","villanelle, but only if it has no narrative","epic catalog listing warriors or ships"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Ballads tell stories in song-like stanzas. A sonnet is 14 lines of argument/lyric. A villanelle is 19 lines with two refrains and is not defined by 'no story.' An epic catalog is a list inside epic, not the whole form." },

  { id:"LT037", domain:"literature", title:"Consonance versus Alliteration", difficulty:"hard", tags:["sound"], skillIds:["sound-devices"],
    stem:"In Blake's fearful symmetry, the repeated r sounds in fearful and symmetry are best labeled—",
    choices:["consonance (repeated consonant sounds not limited to initial position)","alliteration only, because both r sounds begin their words","a volta turning the sonnet's argument","dramatic irony about who framed the Tyger"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Consonance repeats consonants anywhere; alliteration is typically initial—and fearful does not begin with r. A volta is a sonnet turn; irony is a knowledge gap, not a sound pattern." },

  { id:"LT038", domain:"literature", title:"Paradox of Kindly Death", difficulty:"hard", tags:["figurative"], skillIds:["figurative-language"],
    passage:"Emily Dickinson's lyric presents Death as a caller who takes the speaker on a slow carriage ride past school, grain, and sunset:\n\nBecause I could not stop for Death –\nHe kindly stopped for me –\nThe Carriage held but just Ourselves –\nAnd Immortality.\n\nWe slowly drove – He knew no haste\nAnd I had put away\nMy labor and my leisure too,\nFor His Civility –\n\nWe passed the School, where Children strove\nAt Recess – in the Ring –\nWe passed the Fields of Gazing Grain –\nWe passed the Setting Sun –",
    stem:"Kindly Death is paradoxical because—",
    choices:["courtesy is attributed to a force usually feared as an ending","Death is portrayed as a documented historical monarch","the lines form a comic stanza that undercuts any seriousness","the speaker is a chorus explaining the plot to the audience"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Paradox yokes opposites: kindness and death. Death is not a British monarch here; the tone is lyric and eerie, not comic relief; Dickinson's speaker is not a Greek chorus." },

  { id:"LT039", domain:"literature", title:"Free Indirect Style Adjacent", difficulty:"medium", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From Jane Austen, Pride and Prejudice. The narrator opens by treating neighborhood gossip as if it were a law of nature, then shows how families convert a bachelor into a prize:\n\nIt is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.\n\nHowever little known the feelings or views of such a man may be on his first entering a neighbourhood, this truth is so well fixed in the minds of the surrounding families, that he is considered as the rightful property of some one or other of their daughters.\n\n'My dear Mr. Bennet,' said his lady to him one day, 'have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?'",
    stem:"The phrase rightful property of ... their daughters most clearly reveals—",
    choices:["the neighborhood's mercenary, possessive attitude toward a wealthy bachelor","that the man has already married one of the daughters","a storm that has already destroyed Netherfield Park","the narrator's unironic belief that women are legally property"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Property applied to a person exposes how families treat the bachelor as a prize. He has just entered; Netherfield is let, not wrecked. Austen's irony invites us to judge the attitude, not to accept it as the novel's ethic." },

  { id:"LT040", domain:"literature", title:"Sojourner Truth Rhetoric", difficulty:"medium", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    passage:"From Sojourner Truth's 1851 speech as reported in the Anti-Slavery Bugle:\n\nI want to say a few words about this matter. I am a woman's rights. I have as much muscle as any man, and can do as much work as any man. I have plowed and reaped and husked and chopped and mowed, and can any man do more than that? I have heard much about the sexes being equal; I can carry as much as any man, and can eat as much too, if I can get it. I am as strong as any man that is now. As for intellect, all I can say is, if woman have a pint and man a quart—why can't she have her little pint full?",
    stem:"Truth's pint and quart comparison is best read as—",
    choices:["an analogy arguing women should be allowed to develop whatever intellect they have","a concession that women have no minds worth educating","a claim that a pint is literally smaller, so women should stop asking","a couplet that closes a sonnet rather than a speech"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Even if someone grants men a 'larger' mind, she demands that women fill their own measure—an equality argument from lived labor. It is not a denial of intellect, a call to quit, or verse form." },

  { id:"LT041", domain:"literature", title:"Realism in Austen", difficulty:"hard", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    passage:"From Jane Austen, Pride and Prejudice:\n\nIt is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.\n\nHowever little known the feelings or views of such a man may be on his first entering a neighbourhood, this truth is so well fixed in the minds of the surrounding families, that he is considered as the rightful property of some one or other of their daughters.\n\n'My dear Mr. Bennet,' said his lady to him one day, 'have you heard that Netherfield Park is let at last?'",
    stem:"This opening is closer to literary Realism than to high Romantic lyric because it—",
    choices:["satirizes ordinary class motives (money, marriage, neighborhood gossip) rather than visionary nature-rapture","invokes a muse and a ten-year war in the opening sentence","describes a sublime storm that dwarfs human fortune","is a slave narrative's Middle Passage chapter in comic disguise"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Austen's social comedy inspects fortune, marriage markets, and talk—Realist ancestry. Muse/war is epic; sublime storm is Romantic; Equiano's horror is a different genre." },

  { id:"LT042", domain:"literature", title:"Blake's Chimney Sweeper", difficulty:"hard", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From William Blake, 'The Chimney Sweeper' (Songs of Innocence):\n\nWhen my mother died I was very young,\nAnd my father sold me while yet my tongue\nCould scarcely cry ''weep! 'weep! 'weep! 'weep!'\nSo your chimneys I sweep & in soot I sleep.\n\nThere's little Tom Dacre, who cried when his head\nThat curled like a lamb's back, was shaved, so I said,\n'Hush, Tom! never mind it, for when your head's bare,\nYou know that the soot cannot spoil your white hair.'\n\nAnd so he was quiet, & that very night,\nAs Tom was a-sleeping he had such a sight—\nThat thousands of sweepers, Dick, Joe, Ned, & Jack,\nWere all of them locked up in coffins of black.",
    stem:"In social context, the poem protests—",
    choices:["the use of small children in dangerous chimney work, framed in a child's voice","the closing of public parks where Tom might have played","a tax on tea as the only political target of 1773","the invention of the sonnet, which the sweeps cannot read"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Sold, soot, shaved heads, and coffins of black indict child labor. The innocent voice makes the protest sharper. Parks, the Tea Act, and sonnet history are not the target, even if a skimmer grabs 'young' or the date of Blake's era." },

  { id:"LT043", domain:"literature", title:"Tragedy Prologue Function", difficulty:"hard", tags:["drama"], skillIds:["prose-drama-analysis"],
    passage:"From the Prologue to William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet. Before any character speaks, the chorus tells the audience how the feud will end:\n\nTwo households, both alike in dignity,\nIn fair Verona, where we lay our scene,\nFrom ancient grudge break to new mutiny,\nWhere civil blood makes civil hands unclean.\nFrom forth the fatal loins of these two foes\nA pair of star-cross'd lovers take their life;\nWhose misadventured piteous overthrows\nDo with their death bury their parents' strife.\nThe fearful passage of their death-mark'd love,\nAnd the continuance of their parents' rage,\nWhich, but their children's end, nought could remove,\nIs now the two hours' traffic of our stage.",
    stem:"Star-cross'd and death-mark'd mainly suggest that the tragedy is framed as—",
    choices:["fated or doomed love, not a random farce","a courtroom transcript of the families' legal case","a pastoral about peaceful country life in Verona","an essay that will document sources for the feud"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Astrological 'stars' and marked death present destiny and family feud as the engine of tragedy. It is drama, not a legal record, pastoral, or research paper." },

  { id:"LT044", domain:"literature", title:"POV Clash in Tell-Tale Heart", difficulty:"hard", tags:["narrative"], skillIds:["narrative-elements"],
    passage:"From Edgar Allan Poe, 'The Tell-Tale Heart.' The narrator addresses an implied listener and insists on sanity while describing obsession:\n\nTRUE!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.\n\nIt is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!",
    stem:"The narrator's claim to speak healthily and calmly is undercut most by—",
    choices:["exclamations, repetition, and boasts of hearing heaven, earth, and hell","a footnote from a physician confirming the disease","Austen's opening sentence, which this narrator quotes","a missing setting, because no room is named"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Style contradicts content: TRUE!, dashes, repetition, and supernatural hearing do not sound calm. No doctor footnote or Austen narrator appears; setting is implied in the confession itself." },

  { id:"LT045", domain:"literature", title:"Equiano Audience", difficulty:"hard", tags:["world-lit"], skillIds:["world-literature"],
    passage:"From Olaudah Equiano, The Interesting Narrative:\n\nThe closeness of the place, and the heat of the climate, added to the number in the ship, which was so crowded that each had scarcely room to turn himself, almost suffocated us. This produced copious perspirations, so that the air soon became unfit for respiration, from a variety of loathsome smells, and brought on a sickness among the slaves, of which many died, thus falling victims to the improvident avarice, as I may call it, of their purchasers. The shrieks of the women, and the groans of the dying, rendered the whole a scene of horror almost inconceivable.",
    stem:"Equiano's most likely primary audience in 1789 included—",
    choices:["British readers who could be moved toward abolition","only the ship's cargo clerk, as a correction to the manifest","readers seeking a travelogue of climate and crowding with no moral claim","a single theater audience expecting comic relief"], answer:0,
    explanation:"The Interesting Narrative was a public abolitionist autobiography aimed at British conscience. Sensory horror plus avarice names a moral audience, not a clerk, a travelogue, or comic theater." },

  { id:"LT046", domain:"literature", title:"Whitman versus Meter Tradition", difficulty:"hard", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    passage:"From Walt Whitman, 'Song of Myself.' The poet refuses regular rhyme and writes in long, speech-like lines about self, body, and grass:\n\nI celebrate myself, and sing myself,\nAnd what I assume you shall assume,\nFor every atom belonging to me as good belongs to you.\nI loafe and invite my soul,\nI lean and loafe at my ease observing a spear of summer grass.\nMy tongue, every atom of my blood, form'd from this soil, this air,\nBorn here of parents born here from parents the same, and their parents the same,\nI, now thirty-seven years old in perfect health begin,\nHoping to cease not till death.",
    stem:"Whitman's long, unrhymed, speech-like lines are best described as—",
    choices:["free verse that breaks from regular sonnet meter while keeping prophetic cadence","a strict villanelle with two repeating refrains","heroic couplets rhymed throughout in pentameter pairs","a sequence of comic anapestic stanzas"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Song of Myself is foundational free verse: no abab pentameter grid, but biblical/oratorical rhythm. Villanelles, couplets, and light anapestic stanzas have tight schemes he is not using here." },

  { id:"LT047", domain:"literature", title:"Truth's Labor Ethos", difficulty:"hard", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From Sojourner Truth's 1851 speech as reported in the Anti-Slavery Bugle:\n\nI have as much muscle as any man, and can do as much work as any man. I have plowed and reaped and husked and chopped and mowed, and can any man do more than that? I have heard much about the sexes being equal; I can carry as much as any man, and can eat as much too, if I can get it. I am as strong as any man that is now.",
    stem:"In the context of nineteenth-century women's rights and abolition, these sentences mainly build ethos by—",
    choices:["citing embodied labor that contradicts claims women are too weak for public rights","listing legal maxims instead of personal experience","praising factory owners who hired women for the same tasks","denying that plowing and reaping count as work"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Plowed, reaped, husked, chopped, mowed is lived evidence against the 'frail woman' stereotype, spoken by a Black woman who had been enslaved. That intersection is the historical point. Not maxims, owner praise, or denial of work." },

  { id:"LT048", domain:"literature", title:"Period Placement of Poe", difficulty:"hard", tags:["periods"], skillIds:["literary-periods"],
    stem:"Edgar Allan Poe is most accurately grouped with—",
    choices:["American Gothic / Dark Romanticism","High Victorian factory-reform verse only","Augustan mock-epic couplets only","postmodern metafiction of the 1980s"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Poe's terror, obsession, and the supernatural sit in Dark Romantic/Gothic American literature. Barrett Browning's Cry of the Children is Victorian protest; Pope is Augustan; 1980s metafiction is far later." },

  { id:"LT049", domain:"literature", title:"Douglass's Robbers Metaphor", difficulty:"hard", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From Frederick Douglass, Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass. After learning to read, he reinterprets slavery as crime rather than a natural order:\n\nThe more I read, the more I was led to abhor and detest my enslavers. I could regard them in no other light than a band of successful robbers, who had left their homes, and gone to Africa, and stolen us from our homes, and in a strange land reduced us to slavery. I loathed them as being the meanest as well as the most wicked of men. As I read and contemplated the subject, behold! that very discontentment which Master Hugh had predicted would follow my learning to read had already come, to torment and sting my soul to unutterable anguish.",
    stem:"Calling enslavers robbers is a rhetorical move that—",
    choices:["redefines slavery as theft of persons rather than a natural social order","argues Africa had no homelands worth naming","praises the Middle Passage as ordinary trade","is merely decorative and carries no argument"], answer:0,
    explanation:"Robbers and stolen us reject proslavery 'natural order' stories and name a crime. He affirms homes in Africa; he does not praise the trade; the metaphor carries the argument." },

  { id:"LT050", domain:"literature", title:"Children's Weeping as Evidence", difficulty:"hard", tags:["context"], skillIds:["literature-context"],
    passage:"From Elizabeth Barrett Browning, 'The Cry of the Children':\n\nDo ye hear the children weeping, O my brothers,\nEre the sorrow comes with years?\nThey are leaning their young heads against their mothers,\nAnd that cannot stop their tears.\nThe young lambs are bleating in the meadows,\nThe young birds are chirping in the nest,\nThe young fawns are playing with the shadows,\nThe young flowers are blowing toward the west—\nBut the young, young children, O my brothers,\nThey are weeping bitterly!\nThey are weeping in the playtime of the others,\nIn the country of the free.",
    stem:"Country of the free is bitter because it—",
    choices:["exposes a gap between national self-image and children's suffering","proves the poem is set in a scientific study of desert washes","is a stage direction telling actors where to stand","names the children's work route rather than a political irony"], answer:0,
    explanation:"The phrase ironizes a nation that calls itself free while children weep in others' playtime—literature as social critique. It is not hydrology, blocking, or a literal route." }
]);
